 ## Overview
-Superpixel-guide Efficient Sampling (SES) is a training strategy for Neural Radiance Fields (NeRF) and neural fields that uses SLIC superpixels as the smallest processing units to guide ray sampling [S1]. It addresses the slow convergence and scattered sampling issues in high-resolution NeRF training by focusing computational resources on complex regions with high rendering errors [S1]. Unlike quadtree-based methods which suffer from redundant computations and fragmented information due to fixed geometric shapes, SES resolves inadequate partitioning issues and reduces computational overhead per instance by adapting to image content [S1]. The method builds on [Adaptive Ray Sampling](14469-adaptive-ray-sampling.md) by replacing fixed hierarchical partitions with content-aware superpixel masks.
+Superpixel-guide Efficient Sampling (SES) is a training strategy for Neural Radiance Fields (NeRF) and neural fields that uses SLIC superpixels as the smallest processing units to guide ray sampling [S1]. It addresses the slow convergence and scattered sampling issues in high-resolution NeRF training by focusing computational resources on complex regions with high rendering errors [S1]. Unlike quadtree-based methods which suffer from redundant computations and fragmented information due to fixed geometric shapes, SES resolves inadequate partitioning issues and reduces computational overhead per instance by adapting to image content [S1]. The method builds on [Adaptive Ray Sampling](14469-adaptive-ray-sampling.md) by replacing fixed hierarchical partitions with content-aware superpixel masks [S1].
 
